9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-16584985-4e2b-4f1d-9add-5745ea691759-attributes-letterspacing:undefined;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-16584985-4e2b-4f1d-9add-5745ea691759-attributes-texttransform:undefined"><span style="width:100%"><p><strong>See the 3-step plan </strong>→</p></span></div></div></div></button><div data-rid="0e0cf4d9-c7fb-41c7-a17a-17d997d4a6c7" class="r-1hmeydw"><div data-rid="fa5c1d88-f563-41f0-8347-471e214df71a" class="r-1qbngpv alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p><br></p><h2><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)"><strong>What exactly<em> is</em> period pain?</strong></span></h2></span></div><div data-rid="838c2ad4-725d-444f-8be9-c63a709f47cb" class="r-1lfr4c4 alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>&nbsp;"Period pain also called dysmenorrhoea, can be separated into two categories,” explains Ashley&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><ul><li><p>Primary dysmenorrhoea: pain with no known underlying cause. This is the most common type.</p></li><li><p>Secondary dysmenorrhoea: pain caused by a specific condition, such as endometriosis or fibroids. &nbsp;</p></li></ul><p><br></p><p>"The main driver is believed to be prostaglandins, compounds in your body that trigger things like pain, inflammation and uterine contractions.<strong> People who make more prostaglandins tend to experience more period pain."</strong><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)"><strong>&nbsp;</strong></span></p></span></div></div><div data-rid="9227689a-a562-4da6-bd72-b3d38a977bcd" class="r-1qppf5n"><div data-rid="4fe2a725-82e0-4b4e-be36-7c72910e475c" class="r-fio560 alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h2><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">"If you have primary dysmenorrhoea, diet and lifestyle tweaks can make a real difference"</span></h2><p><br></p></span></div><div data-rid="eec8cc8c-9809-4ba1-8218-5a98838b326a" class="r-19hxcuc alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h2><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)"><strong>Can lifestyle changes actually help?</strong></span></h2></span></div><div data-rid="346fbae5-59f3-4c6f-ab75-07d9366e11c6" class="r-njsbz7 alchemy-rte" style="--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-6bbac2cc-d339-470d-9a9c-43ceb03a037a-attributes-color-background-color:#efefef"><span style="width:100%"><p><em>Yes - especially for primary dysmenorrhoea</em></p><p><br></p><p>Ashley shares:<em> </em>"If you have primary dysmenorrhoea, diet and lifestyle can make a real difference"</p><p><br></p><p>Try these science-backed strategies:<br></p><p>• Eat a well-balanced diet rich in fruit, vegetables, healthy fats, protein and fibre.</p><p><br></p><p>&nbsp;• Include plenty of cruciferous vegetables like broccoli, cauliflower, kale and cabbage to support healthy hormone metabolism.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>• Consume omega-3's regularly (salmon, sardines, flaxseed) to help with prostaglandins.<span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">&nbsp;</span></p><p><br></p><p>• Focus on key nutrients: C, D and E, Bs, zinc, ginger, omega-3 and magnesium (when combined with B6)</p><p><br></p><p>• Gentle movement: walking or yoga can ease cramps</p><p><br></p><p>• Prioritise sleep and stress management: poor sleep and chronic stress can worsen PMS and period pain.</p></span></div></div><picture data-rid="f835ede9-7687-487c-8dc1-ebde221a2146" class="r-vhlps5"><source srcSet="https://assets.replocdn.com/projects/05d1c41a-8cc0-4d37-87de-b42dfbdbefe9/14f6baf7-1879-4d44-a50e-23524be93acb?width=820" media="(max-width: 640px)"/><source srcSet="https://assets.replocdn.com/projects/74ef5866-9ad7-456b-81d4-5e84e591ae16/d20ee3f2-4c51-4c94-b523-51a39c091e1b?width=1024" media="(min-width: 641px) and (max-width: 1024px)"/><source srcSet="https://assets.replocdn.com/projects/05d1c41a-8cc0-4d37-87de-b42dfbdbefe9/14f6baf7-1879-4d44-a50e-23524be93acb?width=1800" media="(min-width: 1025px) and (max-width: 2400px)"/><img src="https://assets.replocdn.com/projects/05d1c41a-8cc0-4d37-87de-b42dfbdbefe9/14f6baf7-1879-4d44-a50e-23524be93acb" alt="Wooden spoons with various beans and legumes on a rustic surface." class="r-1bcwgqf" loading="eager"/></picture><div data-rid="3f34fb3d-0aa9-4c47-9a31-85dec0be7f85" class="r-888kc6"><div data-rid="50bc7aa4-59e9-4113-aaa0-8552a1c5c63e" class="r-khv5je"><div data-rid="be43e2cb-d5bb-412a-b36d-bd726a98efe7" class="r-i5sin7 alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h2><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)"><strong>What about PMS?</strong></span></h2></span></div></div><div data-rid="597cde5c-bc09-4a4e-a60b-47975a9f8a21" class="r-o1pu69 alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>"PMS stands for premenstrual syndrome, and these are the physical and mental symptoms you experience in the weeks leading up to your period. For some it can start right after ovulation, for others it can start just a few days before. The symptoms are often mood swings, fatigue, bloating, breast tenderness. </p><br><p>It is really important to know the difference between PMS and PMDD. PMDD stands for Premenstrual Dysphoric Disorder, and this is a much more serious form of PMS that interferes with your health and daily activity. It is critical that you speak to your healthcare provider if you feel this may be you."</p></span></div></div><div data-rid="9fe10d4f-c5c1-4895-9408-28a0b11f8377" class="r-1emtbm4"><div data-rid="8b66d884-abc7-42b5-a66b-96e64059df65" class="r-zmc1sz alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h2><strong>How can we help with other symptoms like PMS?</strong></h2></span></div><div data-rid="599df996-0d66-4f95-b7ab-b5b3cdb11d97" class="r-1x8t1vg alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Cut Back on ultra-processed foods and alcohol both can worsen PMS symptoms.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Eat anti-inflammatory whole foods: whole grains, nuts, legumes and leafy greens.</p><p><br></p><p>Add targeted nutrients:</p><ul><li><p><span>Calcium (dairy, canned salmon with bones, broccoli, sesame seeds)</span><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">&nbsp;</span></p></li><li><p><span>Vitamin B6, saffron, chamomile, and vitamin D supplements (check with your doctor)</span><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">&nbsp;</span></p></li></ul><p><br></p></span></div></div><div data-rid="953e3e31-55b8-4ffa-b391-bb4e032b78c0" data-alchemy-url-hashmark="3Steps" tabindex="-1" id="3Steps" data-alchemy-url-hashmark-ignore="true" class="r-mmqsom"><div data-rid="9623da1c-d85e-4081-83ee-259370e61e61" class="r-f1slqq alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h2><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)"><strong>What can I do <em>during</em> my period (besides painkillers)?</strong></span></h2></span></div><div data-rid="cebcb3bc-8e9c-42c4-9d75-48bb88ab9b45" class="r-1d9dymh alchemy-rte" style="--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-66dce983-a772-4027-acd1-f4dc80952245-attributes-color-background-color:#efefef"><span style="width:100%"><p><strong>"Heat:</strong> A heating pad, warm bath or heat patches can relax muscles and distracts nerves.&nbsp;<span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)"><br></span><strong>Gentle movement: </strong>Walking or yoga can ease cramps.&nbsp;<span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)"><br></span><strong>Acupressure: </strong>Press point SP-6 (about four finger-breadths above the inner ankle bone) for a minute or two.&nbsp;<span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)"><br></span><strong>TENS units:</strong> Electrical nerve stimulation can help.&nbsp;<span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)"><br></span><strong>Aromatherapy: </strong>Lavender essential oil has some minimal evidence behind it.&nbsp;<span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)"><br></span><strong>Supplements:</strong> Zinc, ginger and magnesium may reduce pain when taken during your period."</p><p><br></p></span></div><div data-rid="ee41feb3-fc0a-4009-9322-8fcd772e685f" class="r-cmp86c alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h2><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">"Here's an example of 3 easy steps to add to your routine to start conquering period pain"</span></h2><p><br></p></span></div><div data-rid="065521ea-23ea-4c0d-8a1a-62c6eb37c4d5" class="r-1dr2kfq alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h2><strong>So... where do I start?</strong></h2></span></div><div data-rid="5b6184fe-8757-4357-a863-81246fb03263" class="r-13t7o2h alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Feeling overwhelmed? That's normal.</p><br><p>The key is to pick one or two small changes, weave them into your routine and give them time to stick. </p><br><p>Once they feel second nature, layer in another habit. Those steady, incremental steps are what create lasting results. </p><br><p><strong>A simple 3-step daily routine for period health:</strong></p></span></div><div data-rid="8a206874-e109-4652-a1b0-d1a6411d59c7" class="r-jmag9v"><div data-rid="f5915b76-286d-42d7-9ca4-45f79d3ab466" class="r-1uugaz3"><picture data-rid="e0e1fa1a-edd8-4fce-86aa-d1fac4256a15" class="r-1lmwji1"><source srcSet="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0708/7462/8371/files/header-mob.jpg?v=1741622235&amp;width=100" media="(max-width: 640px)"/><source srcSet="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0771/0860/9337/files/Kuru_17.webp?v=1724611480&amp;width=1024" media="(min-width: 641px) and (max-width: 1024px)"/><source srcSet="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0771/0860/9337/files/Kuru_17.webp?v=1724611480&amp;width=1800" media="(min-width: 1025px) and (max-width: 2400px)"/><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0771/0860/9337/files/Kuru_17.webp?v=1724611480" alt="Close-up of green mint leaves in a garden." class="r-ffm2zf" loading="lazy"/></picture></div><div data-rid="29b85553-3ece-4966-9942-5eb1ad97c02c" class="r-152xert alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p><strong>1. Morning Movement</strong></p></span></div><div data-rid="b39039dd-9cd7-47bc-89a2-677123bffb4f" class="r-4wyd7v alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Take a brisk <strong>20-minute walk</strong> outside to support circulation, your circadian rhythm and stress levels.</p></span></div></div><div data-rid="cef65071-9c5d-48c8-bbca-a8cbbaac7166" class="r-x0srfo"><div data-rid="79113e7f-f4d8-447c-b059-9faff6182828" class="r-q34vkp alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)"><strong>2. Balanced Breakfast</strong></span></p></span></div><div data-rid="70b3c925-5e90-4f00-8df1-cdf4fa182ed4" class="r-szrp4y"><picture data-rid="ecf53360-b7a3-4375-8fea-9ca5e199d998" class="r-1tp8jn6"><source srcSet="https://assets.replocdn.com/projects/05d1c41a-8cc0-4d37-87de-b42dfbdbefe9/a901c162-c96e-4a36-8e57-17a66fa76b53?width=100" media="(max-width: 640px)"/><source srcSet="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0771/0860/9337/files/Kuru_17.webp?v=1724611480&amp;width=1024" media="(min-width: 641px) and (max-width: 1024px)"/><source srcSet="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0771/0860/9337/files/Kuru_17.webp?v=1724611480&amp;width=1800" media="(min-width: 1025px) and (max-width: 2400px)"/><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0771/0860/9337/files/Kuru_17.webp?v=1724611480" alt="Close-up of green mint leaves in a garden." class="r-19dkc1j" loading="lazy"/></picture></div><div data-rid="739c331c-6d99-4b9f-aed0-51e5af6a5e42" class="r-1z0pii0 alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">Include protein, healthy fat, and fibre, e.g.</span></p><p>a) Greek yoghurt topped with berries and mixed seeds &nbsp;</p><p>b) Poached eggs on whole-grain toast with avocado.&nbsp;</p></span></div></div><div data-rid="dbb6cf2c-e9d0-4ce2-8789-01510210b4c0" class="r-i2bo6f"><div data-rid="5995e138-83f4-4c7b-a99d-13856fb11cd0" class="r-l27xc6"><picture data-rid="a45715e5-aec4-45b4-9a81-216c52ab06f5" class="r-1lanq7l"><source srcSet="https://assets.replocdn.com/projects/05d1c41a-8cc0-4d37-87de-b42dfbdbefe9/03b80a18-2055-4ebb-bfa1-8f788219f921?width=100" media="(max-width: 640px)"/><source srcSet="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0771/0860/9337/files/Kuru_17.webp?v=1724611480&amp;width=1024" media="(min-width: 641px) and (max-width: 1024px)"/><source srcSet="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0771/0860/9337/files/Kuru_17.webp?v=1724611480&amp;width=1800" media="(min-width: 1025px) and (max-width: 2400px)"/><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0771/0860/9337/files/Kuru_17.webp?v=1724611480" alt="Close-up of green mint leaves in a garden." class="r-hefcbt" loading="lazy"/></picture></div><div data-rid="f833c79f-d9f2-437f-8229-f449cfe9c68a" class="r-1lrwf7r alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p><strong>3. Daily Scoop</strong></p></span></div><div data-rid="4d6786e4-8e9e-47a4-9ab5-a9bb84f2875b" class="r-5ezobb alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">Here's the truth: <strong>People can't get every needed nutrient from food alone - especially at the dosages we see in studies to relieve pain.</strong></span></p></span></div></div><div data-rid="ea24e3aa-025c-49e2-a3e6-66dfc826d2d1" class="r-skjbx alchemy-rte" style="--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-47d07597-4c33-4196-be19-2bfb2ff4d855-attributes-color-background-color:#8cbfbd"><span style="width:100%"><p><br></p><p>That's why we created <strong>monthlies.</strong></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Meet monthlies: Your once-a-day hormonal support</strong></p><p><br></p><p>monthlies is a complete daily powder combining vitamins, minerals and botanicals specifically chosen for your period health.</p><ul><li><p>Ease intense monthly cramps naturally*</p></li><li><p>Reduce mood swings, bloating and PMS*</p></li><li><p>The UK's first and most complete formula.</p></li><li><p>Just one scoop-a-day</p></li></ul><p><br></p><p><strong>Ready to try it?</strong></p><p><br></p><p>Click here to learn more about monthlies</p></span></div></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="64d37a2b-65c5-4415-8b32-3483d3264e0f" class="r-10xnrh5"><picture data-rid="de69df98-1ef7-421c-a0db-3a1593b5fe72" class="r-1uuev9n"><source srcSet="https://assets.replocdn.com/projects/05d1c41a-8cc0-4d37-87de-b42dfbdbefe9/9368b2b6-ce69-470f-991f-4736ee8e01af?width=820" media="(max-width: 640px)"/><source srcSet="https://assets.replocdn.com/projects/05d1c41a-8cc0-4d37